([SOLVED PROBLEM 1: Beer-Lambert Law Molar Absorptivity and Concentration Analysis]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem: A 1.25 * 10^-4 M solution of an active biotechnology enzyme cofactor exhibits) Tj
T*
(an absorbance of A = 0.625 at lambda_max = 280 nm in a 1.0 cm path-length quartz) Tj
T*
(cuvette. Calculate: \(1\) Molar absorptivity epsilon in L/mol*cm, \(2\) Percentage) Tj
T*
(transmittance \(%T\), \(3\) Concentration of an unknown solution of the same cofactor) Tj
T*
(showing A = 0.250 in the same cell.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Calculate Molar Absorptivity epsilon:) Tj
T*
(A = epsilon * c * l => epsilon = A / \(c * l\)) Tj
T*
(epsilon = 0.625 / [ \(1.25 * 10^-4 mol/L\) * \(1.0 cm\) ] = 0.625 / 1.25 * 10^-4 = 5,000) Tj
T*
(L/mol*cm.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 2: Calculate Percentage Transmittance \(%T\):) Tj
T*
(A = - log10\(T\) => T = 10^{-A} = 10^{-0.625} = 0.2371.) Tj
T*
(%T = T * 100 = 23.71%.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 3: Determine Concentration of Unknown Solution:) Tj
T*
