([SOLVED PROBLEM 2: Hooke's Law Wave Number Calculation for Carbonyl Bond \(C=O\)]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem: Calculate the fundamental vibrational stretching frequency \(wave number nu_bar) Tj
T*
(in cm^-1\) of a carbonyl bond \(C=O\) using Hooke's Law. Given: Force constant k = 12.0 *) Tj
T*
(10^5 dyne/cm; atomic masses: Carbon = 12.0 g/mol, Oxygen = 16.0 g/mol; velocity of light) Tj
T*
(c = 3.0 * 10^10 cm/sec; Avogadro's number N_A = 6.022 * 10^23.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Calculate Atomic Masses in grams:) Tj
T*
(m_C = 12.0 / \(6.022 * 10^23\) = 1.993 * 10^-23 g.) Tj
T*
(m_O = 16.0 / \(6.022 * 10^23\) = 2.657 * 10^-23 g.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 2: Compute Reduced Mass mu:) Tj
T*
(mu = \(m_C * m_O\) / \(m_C + m_O\) = [ \(1.993 * 2.657\) * 10^-46 ] / [ \(1.993 + 2.657\) *) Tj
T*
(10^-23 ]) Tj
T*
(mu = \(5.295 * 10^-46\) / \(4.650 * 10^-23\) = 1.1388 * 10^-23 grams.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 3: Apply Hooke's Law Equation:) Tj
T*
(nu_bar = [ 1 / \(2 * pi * c\) ] * sqrt\( k / mu \)) Tj
T*
(2 * pi * c = 2 * 3.14159 * \(3.0 * 10^10 cm/s\) = 1.88496 * 10^11 cm/s.) Tj
T*
(k / mu = \(12.0 * 10^5 dyne/cm\) / \(1.1388 * 10^-23 g\) = 1.0537 * 10^29 s^-2.) Tj
T*
(sqrt\( k / mu \) = sqrt\( 1.0537 * 10^29 \) = 3.246 * 10^14 s^-1.) Tj
T*
(nu_bar = \(3.246 * 10^14\) / \(1.88496 * 10^11\) = 1,722.0 cm^-1.) Tj
T*
(Conclusion: The calculated stretching wave number \(1,722 cm^-1\) matches the experimental) Tj
T*
(carbonyl absorption range \(1,680-1,750 cm^-1\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
([SOLVED PROBLEM 3: Isotopic Substitution Effect on Vibrational Frequency]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem: The stretching frequency of a C-H bond is observed at 2,900 cm^-1. Calculate) Tj
T*
(the expected stretching frequency of the corresponding C-D \(deuterated\) bond, assuming) Tj
T*
(the bond force constant remains unchanged. \(Atomic weights: C = 12, H = 1, D = 2\).) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Ratio of Reduced Masses:) Tj
T*
(mu_{CH} = \(12 * 1\) / \(12 + 1\) = 12 / 13 = 0.9231 amu.) Tj
T*
(mu_{CD} = \(12 * 2\) / \(12 + 2\) = 24 / 14 = 1.7143 amu.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 2: Frequency Relationship:) Tj
T*
(nu_bar_{CD} / nu_bar_{CH} = sqrt\( mu_{CH} / mu_{CD} \)) Tj
T*
(Ratio = sqrt\( 0.9231 / 1.7143 \) = sqrt\( 0.53846 \) = 0.7338.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 3: Compute Deuterated Frequency:) Tj
T*
(nu_bar_{CD} = 2,900 cm^-1 * 0.7338 = 2,128.0 cm^-1.) Tj
T*
(Conclusion: Deuteration induces an 800 cm^-1 isotope shift into the infrared silent) Tj
T*
(region.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
