T*
(* Q1: State and derive Beer-Lambert's Law, giving its mathematical formula, units,) Tj
T*
(and practical analytical limitations.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Derivation: Consider a parallel beam of monochromatic light of intensity I) Tj
T*
(passing through an absorbing solution of concentration c. Lambert's law states that the) Tj
T*
(rate of decrease of intensity with thickness db is proportional to initial intensity:) Tj
T*
(-dI/db = k1*I. Beer's law states that rate of decrease with concentration is) Tj
T*
(proportional to intensity: -dI/dc = k2*I. Combining both: -dI/I = k*c*db. Integrating) Tj
T*
(between limits I_0 \(entering intensity at b=0\) and I \(transmitted intensity at path) Tj
T*
(length l\): int_{I_0}^I \(-dI/I\) = k*c*int_0^l db => ln\(I_0 / I\) = k*c*l. Converting to) Tj
T*
(base-10 logarithm: log10\(I_0 / I\) = \(k / 2.303\)*c*l = epsilon*c*l. Absorbance A =) Tj
T*
(epsilon*c*l. Units: l is in cm, c is in mol/L, and epsilon is molar absorptivity in) Tj
T*
(L/\(mol*cm\). Limitations: \(1\) True linearity holds only at low concentrations \(<0.01 M\);) Tj
T*
(at high concentrations, electrostatic intermolecular interactions alter refractive) Tj
T*
(index. \(2\) Non-monochromatic stray light. \(3\) Chemical equilibria: changes in) Tj
T*
(association, dissociation, or tautomerism alter absorbing species.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Explain the selection rules and fundamental vibrational modes \(stretching and) Tj
T*
(bending\) in Infrared \(IR\) spectroscopy.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Selection Rule: A molecular vibration absorbs infrared radiation if and only if) Tj
T*
(it produces a periodic oscillation in the permanent molecular electric dipole moment: \(d) Tj
T*
(mu / dq\) != 0. Homonuclear diatomic molecules \(N2, O2\) have zero dipole moment in all) Tj
T*
(vibrational states and are completely IR-inactive. Heteronuclear molecules \(HCl, CO\)) Tj
T*
(change dipole during stretching and are strongly IR-active. Degrees of Freedom: A) Tj
T*
